// U7 L1 Quiz

::U7 L1 Quiz Q01::A supplement seller cites only customer testimonials. What is the strongest evaluation? {=Testimonials cannot establish cause; seek independent controlled evidence.~Testimonials prove effectiveness when many customers agree.~The claim is true because the product is sold publicly.~Price is the only evidence needed.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q02::What does “trace a claim” require? {=Locate the original study, data, or statement and inspect its context.~Read only summaries that repeat the claim.~Count social-media likes.~Ask whether the headline is memorable.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q03::A current government page and a ten-year-old anonymous blog disagree. Which factor most strongly favors the government page? {=Accountable authorship, current evidence, and transparent sourcing.~Its web address is shorter.~It contains more images.~It appears first in one search.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q04::Why does a seller-funded study require additional scrutiny? {=A financial conflict may shape study design or reporting.~Funded research is always false.~Only free products can be studied fairly.~Funding matters more than methods in every case.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q05::Two independent systematic reviews reach similar conclusions. What has increased? {=Corroboration of the conclusion.~Proof that no exceptions exist.~The popularity of the claim only.~The seller’s qualifications.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q06::An app requests location and contact access unrelated to its function. What should a user evaluate? {=Whether the data collection is necessary, secure, and explained.~Whether the icon is attractive.~Whether friends installed it.~Whether it sends frequent notifications.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q07::A provider has a license but advertises treatment outside that license. Best response? {=Verify scope of practice and seek an appropriately qualified provider.~Assume any license covers every treatment.~Judge only by the office design.~Use the service because it is nearby.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q08::A headline says a food “cuts risk by 50%,” from 2 cases to 1 in 10,000. What is missing? {=The absolute risk, which shows the size of the change.~The product color.~A celebrity endorsement.~The number of headline words.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q09::What is the best conclusion when evidence is mixed and limited? {=State uncertainty and avoid a stronger claim than the evidence supports.~Choose the most exciting result.~Treat uncertainty as proof of harm.~Ignore all evidence.}

::U7 L1 Quiz Q10::Why compare a health product with alternatives? {=Safety, cost, evidence, access, and expected benefit may differ.~The most expensive choice is always best.~Alternatives eliminate all uncertainty.~Comparison is needed only for prescriptions.}

